# Student B – Initial Design

## Problem
Develop a decision-making method to select a suitable sorting algorithm for different input conditions.

## Proposed Approach
My approach is to consider the input size, memory availability, and expected performance.

1. For small datasets, Quick Sort can be selected for its practical speed.
2. When stable sorting is needed, select Merge Sort.
3. When extra memory is available and predictable performance is important, select Merge Sort.
4. When in-place sorting is preferred, select Quick Sort.

## Reason
The performance of Quick Sort depends on pivot selection. Poor pivot selection can lead to O(n²) time.

Merge Sort divides the input into equal parts and provides O(n log n) time in all cases.

## Conclusion
The choice of sorting algorithm should depend on the dataset and the performance requirements.
